<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Common Spousal Support Misconceptions</h1>
  <p>Both paying and receiving spouses frequently misunderstand spousal support rules. The most common mistaken beliefs:</p>

  <h2>"Only husbands pay alimony"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Modern statutes are gender-neutral. The higher-earning spouse pays, regardless of gender. The percentage of female payers has grown significantly over the past two decades as more women out-earn their spouses.</p>

  <h2>"Alimony is always permanent"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Permanent alimony is increasingly rare. Most states now favor time-limited rehabilitative or durational support. Permanent alimony typically requires long marriages (often 20+ years) and significant income disparity.</p>

  <h2>"Alimony stops automatically at retirement"</h2>
  <p><strong>Usually no.</strong> Retirement is a basis to seek modification, but it doesn't automatically end alimony. You need to file a modification petition and show good-faith retirement. Some recent state statutes (e.g., New Jersey) have created presumptions in favor of modification at full retirement age.</p>

  <h2>"I can deduct my alimony payments"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong (for post-2018 divorces).</strong> The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act eliminated the federal alimony deduction for divorces finalized after 12/31/2018. Payer pays with after-tax dollars; recipient receives tax-free.</p>

  <h2>"My ex's affair will mean they pay more"</h2>
  <p><strong>Usually no.</strong> Most states have eliminated "fault" as a factor in spousal support. The handful of fault states (some Southern states) may consider it, but the vast majority don't.</p>

  <h2>"My prenup will save me"</h2>
  <p><strong>Maybe.</strong> Properly executed prenups limiting or waiving spousal support are generally enforceable. But unconscionability, lack of disclosure, or duress claims can void them. Get the prenup reviewed by an attorney before the marriage if it matters.</p>

  <h2>"If we just live together unmarried, I can't be sued for support"</h2>
  <p><strong>Usually right in most states.</strong> Cohabitation typically doesn't create spousal-support obligations. But some states (notably California's Marvin doctrine) recognize "palimony" claims for cohabitating couples based on express or implied agreements.</p>

  <h2>"My ex's remarriage automatically ends support"</h2>
  <p><strong>Often right.</strong> Most states automatically terminate spousal support when the recipient remarries (though it may need to be formally addressed). Cohabitation may also reduce or terminate support.</p>

  <h2>"The formula is binding on the judge"</h2>
  <p><strong>Usually no.</strong> Formula guidelines in most states are starting points, not binding rules. Judges can deviate with documented reasoning. This means the same fact pattern can yield different outcomes depending on the judge.</p>

  <h2>"Stay-at-home parents have weak cases"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong &mdash; often the opposite.</strong> Many courts heavily favor stay-at-home parents in long marriages, recognizing the career sacrifice. The non-monetary contribution factor is real and substantial in most jurisdictions.</p>

  <h2>"I can pay support in goods or services"</h2>
  <p><strong>Wrong.</strong> Spousal support must be paid in money. You can structure it as lump-sum, in-kind transfers via property division, or other creative arrangements, but the ongoing support obligation must be monetary.</p>
</BaseLayout>
